# Group-Fork-Contributors License (GFC License)
Version 1.0

This license is designed for free and open-source projects. Its goal is to
respect the original creator, contributors, and those who fork and share
changes.

---

## 1. Freedom to Use
Users may freely use, study, run, modify, and distribute this project,
provided the conditions of this license are met.

## 2. No License Change
The text of this license must not be modified in any way. Any project based
on this project must retain this exact license text.

## 3. Contributors Section (Mandatory)
The name of the original creator and all collaborators who contributed to
the project must be listed in the Contributors section of the GitHub
repository. This section is mandatory and must not be removed.

- Adding new people to Contributors is allowed.
- Even if someone made only a small change, you may add them if you wish
  (not mandatory, except in the fork case described in Section 5).
- Removing names from Contributors is prohibited.
- Even if you collaborated with someone, they must be in Contributors.

## 4. Forking
- The only way to modify this project is to fork it.
- The fork must be linked to the original project and to related forks.
- A fork with no changes compared to the original project is not valid.
- Every fork must have at least one meaningful difference from the original
  project.

## 5. Using Other People's Forks
If you use code from someone else's fork in your project:
- You must add their name(s) to the Contributors section of your project.
- You must include the link to their fork repository in your project's
  README.md.
- If your project is based on two or more forks, you may (optionally) add a
  Credits section in README.md, but the Contributors section is mandatory.

## 6. Fork Naming
- A fork's name must not follow the pattern `"name"_better`, because this
  implies the original project was bad. This pattern is only allowed for the
  original project itself (i.e., if the original creator upgrades their
  project).
- Using the pattern `"name"_plus` is allowed, because it indicates
  collaboration and improvement and is not an insult to the original project.
- If you upgrade your project to a separate repository, the old project must
  remain in the fork list and you must write at the end of its description:
  `(older version of this)`

## 7. Monetization and Sales
- The original project may optionally receive financial support via Sponsor
  or Donate. This is allowed.
- Forked projects can never be purchased or be paid.
- Selling the project (original or fork) is prohibited under any
  circumstances.
- The purpose of this project is learning, sharing, and collaboration, not
  profit.

## 8. README.md (Mandatory)
- Every project (original and fork) must have a README.md file.
- The content of README.md must be related to the project.
- In the README.md of forked projects, the link to the original project and
  related forks (if used) must be mentioned.
- Do not put fake fork links unless you have actually used them.

## 9. Professional Behavior
- In any file, message, commit, repository name, or description, there must
  be no bad language, insults, or inappropriate content.
- Do not put irrelevant or fake fork links in README unless you have actually
  used them.

## 10. Contact
If you have any questions, concerns, or issues regarding this license,
please contact via Telegram:

**Telegram:** [@DreamerFALL](https://t.me/DreamerFALL)

Please behave respectfully. The goal of this license is cooperation and
mutual respect, not trouble.

---

This license may be used by anyone for their own projects, provided the text
remains unchanged.
